 writings enabled conjectural history while producing new historiographical concepts and identities via specific narratives and institutions in Spain and Spanish America.","confidence":0.76},"academicOrientation":{"score":72,"confidence":0.7},"profileConfidence":0.78,"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"7a335e0da0ea11271bcf04d6fbc117231d281412877c50e77bf4c63ed956393e","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"History","secondarySubjects":["American History","World History"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily explores historiographical concepts and intellectual critiques within the context of the Atlantic World, thus best fitting the subject of History, with significant relevance to both American and World History.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"fc6b9250e24a7e68dffd61581b277c939b8fea3620145e082f7c6561006a635c","summary":"\"The author traces the cultural processes that led early-modern intellectuals on both sides of the Atlantic to question primary sources that had long been considered authoritative: Mesoamerican codices, early colonial Spanish chronicles, and travel accounts. In the process, he demonstrates how the writings of these critics led to the rise of the genre of conjectural history. The book also adds to the literature on nation formation by exploring the creation of specific identities in Spain and Spanish America by means of particular historical narratives and institutions. Finally, it demonstrates that colonial intellectuals went beyond mirroring or contesting European ideas and put forth daring and original critiques of European epistemologies that resulted in substantially new historiographical concepts.\"--BOOK JACKET.","pageCount":450,"publicationYear":2001,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"History","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-how-to-write-the-history-of-the-new-wor